ostrya (0.11.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A command that writes refs in one transaction checks each ref path
    under the update lock before it writes the detached metadata and the
    first ref. A path that names a directory, that passes through a ref
    file, or that names a special file stops the command, and no ref
    changes. Before this release, commit --branch B --tag T with a
    directory at the path of T moved B and then failed.

 -- Alexey Naidyonov <alexey.naidyonov@gmail.com>  Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:16:32 +0100

ostrya (0.11.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add the command media, an extension of ostrya. media export MOUNT
    REF... copies the commits of collection refs into the media
    repository MOUNT/.ostree/repo, in the layout that ostree create-usb
    writes. media import finds the media repository on the mounted
    volumes, or under --mount PATH, and pulls collection refs from it.
    The trust of the matched remote of each collection checks its
    commits.
  * refs -c lists each ref of a remote under the collection-id of the
    remote. A remote with no valid collection-id, or with the
    collection-id of the repository, gives no row. A malformed escape
    sequence in a collection-id does not make the command fail.
  * refs -c --delete deletes no ref of a remote, and does not check the
    names of the refs of a remote.
  * refs -A --create reports a ref path that names a directory, or that
    passes through a ref file, as a ref that does not exist.
  * A ref entry that is a FIFO or a socket is no ref. The ref listing of
    refs, summary -u, and serve does not wait on a FIFO.
  * pull-local --untrusted hashes each commit that it reads for the
    checks and for the walk of the chain, and each dirtree that the walk
    of the tree reads, before it uses the bytes. It stages the bytes
    that it hashed. pull --collection does the same for a file://
    candidate whose keys check signatures.

 -- Alexey Naidyonov <alexey.naidyonov@gmail.com>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 21:48:57 +0100

ostrya (0.10.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add the global option --remotes-config-dir. The command reads the
    remotes in the NAME.conf files of the remotes directory. It selects
    the directory in this order: the option, the key [ex-ostrya]
    remotes-config-dir, /etc/ostree/remotes.d for the system repository,
    and no directory. init --remotes-config-dir writes the key.
  * remote add writes the remote into the new file NAME.conf of the
    remotes directory, unless [core] add-remotes-config-dir is false.
    remote add --force and remote delete change the file that holds the
    remote. remote delete removes a .conf file that then holds no remote.
  * The GPG keys of a remote are in NAME.trustedkeys.gpg of the
    repository, or, when that file is absent, of the remotes directory.
  * remote gpg-import reads each -k file as a binary or an ASCII-armored
    keyring by itself. A mix of binary and armored files imports.
  * remote gpg-import and remote add --gpg-import refuse key input over
    4 MiB before they read all of it.
  * remote gpg-import and remote add --gpg-import read the repository
    keyring through a symlink, and take a symlink to no file as no
    keyring. They refuse a repository keyring over 4 MiB before they read
    it.
  * The composefs image of a commit is the file
    extensions/ostrya/composefs/AA/REST.cfs. No pull, push, send,
    receive, static delta, or bucket operation carries it. A static delta
    or a push stream with a .cfs object is refused.
  * fsck checks each composefs image against the key
    ostree.composefs.digest.v0 of its commit, and --delete removes a
    faulty image.
  * With [ex-ostrya] generate-composefs true, commit, pull, pull-local,
    receive, the receive endpoint of serve, and static-delta
    apply-offline build and check the composefs image of each complete
    commit that carries ostree.composefs.digest.v0. pull and pull-local
    write a warning when the repository does not seal its objects with
    fs-verity. fsck writes a line for a commit with the key and no image.
  * The collection-id of a repository is its local namespace. refs -c
    --create and pull --collection write refs/heads/REF for that id.
  * refs -c, summary -u, and the ref listing of serve ignore
    refs/mirrors/ID/ of the own collection id. serve serves no file
    under that directory. fsck prints a line for each ref there, and
    prune keeps its commit.
  * push to a bucket and remote summary -u skip the keys of
    refs/mirrors/ID/ of the collection id of the bucket. remote fsck
    gives each such key a note, and remote prune keeps its commit.
  * A revision takes @ID:NAME, the ref NAME of the collection ID. An
    error names a collection ref as @ID:NAME.
  * commit -b and --tag take @ID:NAME. The refs of one commit are in one
    namespace, and ostree.collection-binding holds its id. --tag refuses
    a remote ref.
  * refs --create and refs --delete take @ID:NAME without -c. A PREFIX
    @ID: matches each ref of the collection ID, and a listing prints
    @ID:NAME. refs --delete refuses a PREFIX @ID: with an empty rest, as
    it refuses REMOTE:. -A refuses a collection ref of another id.
  * refs --delete checks each name that a PREFIX selects before it
    deletes a ref of that PREFIX. A name that is not a valid local or
    remote ref, such as test/x:y, makes the command fail, and the PREFIX
    deletes no ref.
  * prune --only-branch and --retain-branch-depth take @ID:NAME.
  * prune --only-branch reads a ref name. It refuses a checksum and
    main^.
  * pull-local takes @ID:REF operands of more than one collection.
  * push, push --onto, push-tree, and send take @ID:NAME for each ref
    that they write. The push protocol carries the namespace of each
    ref, and the server writes @ID:NAME of its own collection id to
    refs/heads/NAME. Each push refuses a ref that is a local name of 64
    lowercase hexadecimal characters on the destination.
  * Add --disable-verify-bindings to push, send, and receive, and the
    client group key disable-verify-bindings. They skip the check of
    ostree.ref-binding.
  * receive --one-way takes --allow-collection=ID.
  * pull from a named remote takes REF and @ID:REF operands of more than
    one collection. @ID:REF writes refs/mirrors/ID/REF, or
    refs/heads/REF for the own collection id.
  * pull --mirror writes a plain REF as @S:REF, where S is the collection
    id of the summary. With no ref, it pulls each ref of the ref list and
    of the collection map of the summary.
  * pull and pull-local refuse two operands that write one ref.
  * pull --collection=ID takes REF and @ID:REF. A remote is a candidate
    when its collection-id is ID or its mirrored-collection-ids key holds
    ID. remote add takes --mirrored-collection-id.
  * A client group holds the items of each namespace: allow takes
    [MODES:]@ID:PATTERN, and deny takes @ID:PATTERN. The config refuses
    the key collection, and one item in two groups. With no client
    group, the one implicit group is anonymous.
  * A claim is ostrya:ref:[MODES:]@ID:PATTERN or ostrya:ref:!@ID:PATTERN
    for the collection ID. serve refuses a certificate with an
    ostrya:collection: URI with 403.

 -- Alexey Naidyonov <alexey.naidyonov@gmail.com>  Sat, 07 Nov 2026 22:26:49 +0100

ostrya (0.9.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * pull --collection reads a file:// candidate as a local repository.
    It hashes each object it imports from such a candidate when the keys
    of the candidate check the signatures of the commits or the summary.
  * pull --collection skips a listed ref whose name fails the ref-name
    rule, with a warning.
  * pull --collection prints the pull-local statistics line for the
    objects it imports from local candidates.
  * pull --collection under the own collection id writes
    refs/mirrors/ID/REF when that file holds a ref, and refs/heads/REF
    otherwise.
  * refs -c --delete checks each ref before it deletes a ref, and
    refuses a local name with ':'.

